-- Numeric: the ARITHMETIC BUNDLE — `Additive` + `Multiplicative` + a comparison surface.
--
-- WHAT IT ALREADY CALLED ITSELF IS NOW ITS JOB. `algebra.anthill` describes this sort as
-- "Ring + Ord bundled into one spec for primitive arithmetic types" — a convenience
-- beside the algebra rather than in it, written as an apology. Under the syntax-category
-- rule (WI-20260825-1WBZT, `arithmetic.anthill`) that becomes the design: the operations
-- are DECLARED by the categories that mint them, and this spec's whole content is the
-- claim that a carrier has all of them at once. A primitive arithmetic type says
-- `provides Numeric[T = C]` and gets `+ - *` and the comparisons in one row.
--
-- IT DECLARES NOTHING, and that is what makes it a bundle rather than a rival. Before the
-- split it declared `add` / `sub` / `mul` / `neg` / `zero-val` itself, which forced any
-- carrier that could only add to claim a multiplication it did not have — and made
-- `Numeric.add` and `Ring.add` two different operations under one spelling. Both are gone
-- with the declarations; `arithmetic.anthill`'s header carries the measurement.
--
-- `provides`, NOT `requires`, for the reason WI-1110 measured on `Eq`/`PartialEq` and
-- `Ord`/`WeakOrd`: a `provides` on a spec is a CONVERSION — hold a `Numeric[T]` and you
-- can obtain an `Additive[T]` — so the four existing providers (`anthill-stl` int64 /
-- bigint / float, `anthill-cpp-gen` int64) keep their one row and reach every category
-- through the chain, with no edit to any of them. The opposite spelling would make each
-- write three rows and be trusted to keep them consistent.
--
-- ONE COST, ON A POPULATION THE FOUR-PROVIDER CENSUS DID NOT COVER: a NON-HOST carrier
-- claiming this bundle now owes `Multiplicative.one`, an operation `Numeric` never had.
-- `check_provider_operations` walks DERIVED provision rows, and it skips the four
-- bindings above wholesale (they are host targets) — so the census that found "no
-- per-carrier edit" was taken over exactly the population the change cannot break.
-- MEASURED on the pre-split `Money` fixture (add/sub/mul/neg/zero-val + four comparisons,
-- `fact Numeric[T = Money]`): it now fails with TWO errors, "backs no operation
-- 'anthill.prelude.Additive.zero'" and "…'anthill.prelude.Multiplicative.one'", and
-- renaming `zero-val` to `zero` leaves the second. `wi_vt8cf_division_tower_test`'s
-- carrier gained `operation one() -> Money` for that reason.
--
-- THE DEMAND IS THE BUNDLE BEING HONEST, not a regression to absorb: `Numeric` says a
-- carrier has the whole arithmetic surface, and a multiplicative monoid has an identity.
-- A carrier that does not want to owe it claims the categories it can back — which is
-- what this ticket exists to make possible, and what `+`-only carriers should now do.
-- Recorded rather than left to be discovered, because the error arrives at LOAD on a
-- program that used to load.
sort anthill.prelude.Numeric

  sort T = ?
  -- WI-644 / proposal 004: Numeric requires only the PARTIAL order — IEEE Float is
  -- Numeric but not totally Ord (NaN is unordered). Numeric itself uses no
  -- ordering op; the requirement is the comparison surface consumers rely on.
  --
  -- IT STAYS HERE rather than moving to a category, and the split is why it can: a
  -- consumer that wants arithmetic AND comparison asks for `Numeric` and gets both,
  -- while a carrier that only adds asks for `Additive` and owes no comparison at all.
  -- Before the split there was no second address to offer, so this requirement reached
  -- every `+` in the language.
  requires PartialOrd[T]

  provides Additive[T = T]
  provides Multiplicative[T = T]
end
